WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.306
A payments service writes every event to the end of one file, a log.

2
00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:08.931
It never changes a line.

3
00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:10.421
It only adds new ones.

4
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:12.074
Seven records so far.

5
00:00:12.074 --> 00:00:18.175
Three payments are each authorized, then captured, and the last record refunds pay-1.

6
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:22.197
To find one payment in the log, you'd read it from the top.

7
00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:26.871
So the service builds an index: each payment id, with its latest status.

8
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.517
Daily totals feed the merchant dashboard.

9
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:33.212
The refund counts on the day it happened, the third of March.

10
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.487
And a cache keeps the balance of m-cafe, so the dashboard doesn't add it up again on every refresh.

11
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.720
The log is the system of record.

12
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.749
Each fact is written there first, and only once.

13
00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:50.429
The index, the totals and the cache are derived data: all three are computed from the log.

14
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.607
Now the machine that holds the index restarts.

15
00:00:53.607 --> 00:00:56.257
The index lived in memory, and it's gone.

16
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.902
Nothing is lost, because the log still has every fact.

17
00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:04.928
Start an empty index, go back to the first record, and replay the log in order.

18
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.414
Records 0 and 1 authorize pay-1 and pay-2.

19
00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:10.703
Records 2 and 3 capture them.

20
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.453
Records 4 and 5 authorize and capture pay-3.

21
00:01:14.453 --> 00:01:18.167
Record 6, the refund, marks pay-1 as refunded.

22
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:25.145
The rebuilt index gives the same answers as the one that was lost, and nobody typed anything in by hand.

23
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.500
The order matters.

24
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.908
Replay the refund before the capture, and pay-1 would end up captured.

25
00:01:30.908 --> 00:01:34.624
So the log keeps every record, in the order it was written.

26
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.605
Now ask the same of the log.

27
00:01:36.605 --> 00:01:39.182
If it's gone, nothing can bring it back.

28
00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:42.469
The index knows statuses, and the totals know sums.

29
00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:44.789
Neither knows what each payment was.

30
00:01:44.967 --> 00:01:47.872
So write each fact to the system of record first.

31
00:01:47.872 --> 00:01:51.785
Every view derived from it can be thrown away, and computed again.
